00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:14,800
All right.

66
00:02:15,220 --> 00:02:17,780
Now let's go and see whether there is a mixed strategy

67
00:02:17,780 --> 00:02:18,400
Nash equilibria.

68
00:02:18,860 --> 00:02:19,500
Hit. There is.

69
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:20,420
In fact, there is one.

70
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:21,140
All right.

71
00:02:21,140 --> 00:02:23,220
So let us, so what the two,

72
00:02:23,220 --> 00:02:24,820
there are two questions we need to ask.

73
00:02:25,380 --> 00:02:27,580
When is player 2 indifferent between playing left

74
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:28,140
and playing right?

75
00:02:28,940 --> 00:02:31,600
And then when is player 1 indifferent between playing up

76
00:02:31,600 --> 00:02:32,120
and playing down?

77
00:02:33,120 --> 00:02:33,340
All right.

78
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:36,200
So player 2 is going to be indifferent between playing left

79
00:02:36,200 --> 00:02:38,980
and playing right under the following conditions.

80
00:02:38,980 --> 00:02:44,120
So let the probability of up be equal to sigma.

81
00:02:44,800 --> 00:02:47,500
And then of course, down is going to be equal

82
00:02:47,500 --> 00:02:51,040
to 1 minus sigma, because there's only two things.

83
00:02:51,040 --> 00:02:51,440
They can do.

84
00:02:52,400 --> 00:02:55,960
Okay. So then player 2 will be indifferent as long

85
00:02:55,960 --> 00:03:05,620
as 10 times sigma plus 0 times 1 minus sigma equals 15 times

86
00:03:05,620 --> 00:03:09,060
sigma minus 5 times 1 minus sigma.

87
00:03:09,960 --> 00:03:11,300
Why? All right.

88
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:12,640
So now we're trying to find

89
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:14,100
out when player 2 will be indifferent.

90
00:03:14,660 --> 00:03:17,120
So if player 1 is playing up with probability sigma,

91
00:03:17,120 --> 00:03:18,720
down with probability 1 minus sigma,

92
00:03:18,720 --> 00:03:23,420
then if player 1 plays left, he gets 10 with probability sigma

93
00:03:24,240 --> 00:03:26,220
and 0 with probability 1 minus sigma.

94
00:03:26,940 --> 00:03:28,280
So that's 10 with probability sigma,

95
00:03:28,280 --> 00:03:29,580
0 with probability 1 minus sigma.

96
00:03:29,580 --> 00:03:31,560
So that's the first side of the equation, the left side.

97
00:03:32,180 --> 00:03:36,980
On the other hand, if player 2 plays down, excuse me,

98
00:03:36,980 --> 00:03:39,900
so let's see, excuse me, if player 2 plays right,

99
00:03:40,480 --> 00:03:43,380
then he's going to get 15 with probability sigma

100
00:03:44,200 --> 00:03:46,300
and negative 5 with probability 1 minus sigma.

101
00:03:46,300 --> 00:03:49,380
15 with probability sigma, negative 5

102
00:03:49,380 --> 00:03:50,440
with probability 1 minus sigma.

103
00:03:51,540 --> 00:03:51,720
All right.

104
00:03:51,720 --> 00:03:53,620
So do you see where all the numbers are coming from?

105
00:03:54,740 --> 00:03:54,960
All right.

106
00:03:54,960 --> 00:03:58,340
So this is what, so this is what is expected value

107
00:03:58,340 --> 00:04:00,900
from playing left, and that's the expected value

108
00:04:00,900 --> 00:04:01,520
from playing right.

109
00:04:01,520 --> 00:04:04,340
And we're now going to find what value

110
00:04:04,340 --> 00:04:05,640
of sigma makes him indifferent.

111
00:04:06,600 --> 00:04:10,380
And it turns out sigma equals.5 makes him indifferent.

112
00:04:11,340 --> 00:04:13,820
But we don't have a mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium yet

113
00:04:14,320 --> 00:04:15,840
because we've only found out the probability

114
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:16,980
that makes one player different.

115
00:04:16,980 --> 00:04:19,040
We need to have probabilities that make everybody different.

116
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:21,500
It's only two players, so this is one more do.

117
00:04:22,060 --> 00:04:22,360
All right.

118
00:04:22,620 --> 00:04:26,780
So now we need to find, so when player 1 is indifferent

119
00:04:26,780 --> 00:04:27,820
between up and down.

120
00:04:28,400 --> 00:04:34,720
So let player 2's probability of playing left be equal to 5,

121
00:04:34,720 --> 00:04:38,460
and his probability of playing right, then of course,

122
00:04:38,460 --> 00:04:40,260
is going to be 1 minus 5.

123
00:04:41,120 --> 00:04:45,700
Okay. Now we can go and just do a simple alpha problem,

124
00:04:45,700 --> 00:04:49,240
one equation and one unknown, where we will have,

125
00:04:49,700 --> 00:04:56,960
see let me put it up here, 8 phi plus 1 times 1 minus 5,

126
00:04:57,960 --> 00:05:03,300
must equal 12 phi minus 9 times 1 minus 5.

127
00:05:03,640 --> 00:05:06,120
Okay. And again, where is this coming from?

128
00:05:06,120 --> 00:05:07,160
So now player 1.

129
00:05:07,740 --> 00:05:12,480
So player 1, if he plays up, will get 8 with probability phi,

130
00:05:13,340 --> 00:05:15,180
1 with probability 1 minus phi.

131
00:05:15,180 --> 00:05:16,800
So that's the first half of the equation.

132
00:05:17,580 --> 00:05:20,280
If he plays down, he will get 12 with probability phi,

133
00:05:20,740 --> 00:05:22,740
and negative 9 with probability 1 minus phi.

134
00:05:23,400 --> 00:05:24,500
That's the other part of the equation.

135
00:05:25,160 --> 00:05:29,720
And again, if we solve that, then we get phi equals 5 7's.

136
00:05:30,720 --> 00:05:32,680
So the mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium

137
00:05:32,680 --> 00:05:52,320
for this game is going to be sigma phi equals.5 comma 5 7's.

138
00:05:54,040 --> 00:05:54,220
All right.

139
00:05:54,220 --> 00:05:56,760
So when player 1 plays up with probability.5,

140
00:05:57,300 --> 00:05:59,360
and players who plays left with probability 5 7's,

141
00:05:59,360 --> 00:06:02,160
neither player can do better by changing the mix.

142
00:06:04,020 --> 00:06:05,560
Right now, they wouldn't do worse either,

143
00:06:06,140 --> 00:06:07,560
because they both made a difference.

144
00:06:08,120 --> 00:06:10,980
But at any other probabilities,

145
00:06:11,120 --> 00:06:13,280
one player could do better by changing his mix.

146
00:06:13,640 --> 00:06:14,600
These are the only players

147
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:17,080
where neither one has any affirmative incentive

148
00:06:17,080 --> 00:06:20,040
to change, where anyone gets anything extra by changing.

149
00:06:20,580 --> 00:06:22,500
At any other probabilities, at least one

150
00:06:22,500 --> 00:06:25,020
of the players would want to go and change their probabilities.

151
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:28,600
Questions? Questions?

152
00:06:29,740 --> 00:06:31,260
Questions? All right.

153
00:06:32,220 --> 00:06:34,180
Now, now is the part where I try to convince you

154
00:06:34,180 --> 00:06:36,640
that this concept not only makes sense, but makes more sense

155
00:06:36,640 --> 00:06:38,060
than anything else we've done today, actually.

156
00:06:38,880 --> 00:06:39,160
All right.

157
00:06:39,300 --> 00:06:42,560
So, and by that I mean, it's more useful

158
00:06:42,560 --> 00:06:45,080
in the real world, actually illuminates new things.

159
00:06:45,640 --> 00:06:46,700
You know, like all that stuff

160
00:06:46,700 --> 00:06:48,960
that a good scientific hypothesis is supposed to do

161
00:06:48,960 --> 00:06:52,580
for you, like novelty, you know, so forth.

162
00:06:53,060 --> 00:06:53,280
All right.

163
00:06:53,280 --> 00:06:54,940
So, anyway, a lot of people find it bizarre,

164
00:06:55,240 --> 00:06:58,300
and especially in pure theory, there's a lot of hostility

165
00:06:58,300 --> 00:07:00,760
to mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

166
00:07:01,140 --> 00:07:04,800
because pure theorists are basically mathematicians,

167
00:07:05,200 --> 00:07:07,200
and mathematicians on some level don't

168
00:07:07,200 --> 00:07:08,540
like anything other than certainty.

169
00:07:09,140 --> 00:07:12,020
So, when you come up with a list of, when you say, look,

170
00:07:12,020 --> 00:07:12,900
we can't give you an answer,

171
00:07:12,900 --> 00:07:14,780
but we can give you a distribution of probabilities,

172
00:07:15,280 --> 00:07:18,380
standard mathematician is like, it's not a real answer.

173
00:07:19,020 --> 00:07:21,540
A real answer would be a number, not a distribution.

174
00:07:21,540 --> 00:07:25,780
But why? You just don't like it.

175
00:07:26,200 --> 00:07:26,640
So, like it.

176
00:07:26,640 --> 00:07:29,680
I mean, even though, you know, Nash, of course,

177
00:07:29,680 --> 00:07:32,220
a lot of his theorems actually, in fact,

178
00:07:32,620 --> 00:07:35,980
are about mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

179
00:07:36,800 --> 00:07:40,560
because in fact, so as you notice

180
00:07:40,560 --> 00:07:42,080
with pure strategy Nash equilibrium,

181
00:07:42,340 --> 00:07:45,080
you often aren't able to go, and you know, it's quite possible

182
00:07:45,080 --> 00:07:47,100
that a pure strategy Nash equilibrium doesn't even exist.

183
00:07:47,100 --> 00:07:52,820
So, what Nash actually shows is that there will always be a Nash

184
00:07:52,820 --> 00:07:56,300
equilibrium, but if there isn't a pure strategy one,

185
00:07:56,300 --> 00:07:58,400
then it will have to be a mixed strategy one.

186
00:07:59,480 --> 00:08:01,600
So, that's a big part of the theorems

187
00:08:01,600 --> 00:08:04,600
that Nash actually proves, is a Nash equilibrium will exist,

188
00:08:05,100 --> 00:08:07,080
though if a pure strategy does not exist,

189
00:08:07,080 --> 00:08:08,040
then it will be a mixed strategy.

190
00:08:09,180 --> 00:08:10,860
So, he doesn't show that a mixed strategy is always a

191
00:08:10,860 --> 00:08:14,300
mixed strategy, but he does show that if there is,

192
00:08:14,300 --> 00:08:16,160
if a pure strategy does not exist,

193
00:08:16,160 --> 00:08:18,260
then a mixed strategy will exist, again, under, of course,

194
00:08:18,260 --> 00:08:21,220
a list of assumptions, which, however, for this are quite weak.

195
00:08:22,060 --> 00:08:25,160
I don't think Nash actually called his own Nash equilibrium.

196
00:08:25,160 --> 00:08:26,520
I'm not sure about this.

197
00:08:28,740 --> 00:08:30,480
Okay. All right.

198
00:08:30,680 --> 00:08:33,400
So, anyway, what I say is that the whole idea

199
00:08:33,400 --> 00:08:34,880
of mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

200
00:08:34,880 --> 00:08:38,160
it accommodates the strategic complexity of the real world,

201
00:08:38,160 --> 00:08:41,040
you know, when there's interaction

202
00:08:41,040 --> 00:08:41,860
with small numbers of people.

203
00:08:41,860 --> 00:08:42,900
So, think about it this way.

204
00:08:42,900 --> 00:08:46,460
You make your opponents indifferent so that, you know,

205
00:08:46,460 --> 00:08:48,880
so that there's nothing, there's nothing in your behavior

206
00:08:48,880 --> 00:08:49,600
that they can exploit.

207
00:08:50,440 --> 00:08:52,080
So, for example, think about sports.

208
00:08:52,080 --> 00:08:54,000
Suppose that you're playing a game like racquetball.

209
00:08:54,620 --> 00:08:54,900
All right.

210
00:08:54,900 --> 00:08:58,120
Now, what happens if a person always goes and serves the ball

211
00:08:58,120 --> 00:08:59,440
so it shows up in the left corner?

212
00:09:00,200 --> 00:09:01,720
Where does their opponent go and wait for the ball?

213
00:09:02,080 --> 00:09:02,560
The left corner.

214
00:09:02,660 --> 00:09:03,180
The left corner.

215
00:09:03,520 --> 00:09:05,060
What if they always put it in the right corner,

216
00:09:05,340 --> 00:09:07,020
or the middle center, or whatever?

217
00:09:07,440 --> 00:09:09,080
So, if there is a predictable pattern,

218
00:09:09,500 --> 00:09:11,540
then the other person will go and see you and exploit it.

219
00:09:11,540 --> 00:09:13,740
So, what is it that a good player does?

220
00:09:14,920 --> 00:09:15,740
They randomize.

221
00:09:16,200 --> 00:09:18,720
They actually try to eliminate patterns

222
00:09:18,720 --> 00:09:19,920
of behavior that could be exploited.

223
00:09:20,780 --> 00:09:22,880
Once both players have eliminated the patterns

224
00:09:22,880 --> 00:09:24,400
that we exploited, we have a pure strategy

225
00:09:24,400 --> 00:09:25,020
Nash equilibrium.

226
00:09:26,060 --> 00:09:26,280
All right.

227
00:09:26,980 --> 00:09:28,680
So, you know, you can see this in things

228
00:09:28,680 --> 00:09:30,620
like do you hit the ball hard or soft?

229
00:09:30,620 --> 00:09:32,480
If you always hit it hard, you just back up

230
00:09:32,480 --> 00:09:34,860
so that you're ready to get it when they're far distance.

231
00:09:35,140 --> 00:09:37,120
If you always hit it soft, then you scoot in.

232
00:09:37,120 --> 00:09:42,140
But if you randomize, then you've eliminated a pattern

233
00:09:42,140 --> 00:09:42,900
that can be exploited.

234
00:09:43,960 --> 00:09:44,160
All right.

235
00:09:44,160 --> 00:09:46,400
So, you can see this in many kinds of sports

236
00:09:47,140 --> 00:09:49,700
where you look around and say, why are they doing that?

237
00:09:50,180 --> 00:09:51,520
Why don't they all just do this thing?

238
00:09:51,900 --> 00:09:52,740
Well, if they did that thing,

239
00:09:52,740 --> 00:09:54,360
there would be a simple strategic response to it.

240
00:09:54,860 --> 00:09:56,120
So, that's why they don't do that thing.

241
00:09:56,120 --> 00:09:58,740
Instead, you do things that are unpredictable

242
00:09:59,080 --> 00:10:00,900
or that are a response to average conditions

243
00:10:00,900 --> 00:10:02,600
in a way that you can't be exploited.

244
00:10:02,600 --> 00:10:04,120
All right.

245
00:10:04,120 --> 00:10:06,940
Or you can see this in a lot of strategy games.

246
00:10:07,360 --> 00:10:11,040
So, in many strategy games, if you always do the same thing,

247
00:10:11,040 --> 00:10:14,100
it is very easy to defend against it and to beat you.

248
00:10:15,200 --> 00:10:15,400
All right.

249
00:10:15,400 --> 00:10:17,160
So, anyone play the game diplomacy?

250
00:10:18,040 --> 00:10:20,560
Yes. So, if you know what your opponent's moves are,

251
00:10:20,560 --> 00:10:23,020
and you're a decent player, you can win all the time.

252
00:10:24,180 --> 00:10:27,120
Yes. So, once when my kids were really little, I said, all right,

253
00:10:27,120 --> 00:10:28,220
look, I'll give you five countries.

254
00:10:28,220 --> 00:10:30,160
You give me two, but you have to move first,

255
00:10:30,160 --> 00:10:31,060
and I will still beat you.

256
00:10:31,060 --> 00:10:33,040
All right.

257
00:10:33,040 --> 00:10:34,240
Yes, they couldn't do anything to me,

258
00:10:34,240 --> 00:10:35,520
because whatever they did, I was like, well,

259
00:10:35,520 --> 00:10:36,900
just block exactly what you did

260
00:10:36,900 --> 00:10:38,360
and ruin every move you have.

261
00:10:38,880 --> 00:10:39,200
All right.

262
00:10:39,560 --> 00:10:41,920
So, but again, the game is normally played simultaneously.

263
00:10:42,260 --> 00:10:45,800
But if you could predict what the other side was going to do,

264
00:10:45,860 --> 00:10:47,360
which was the point of my exercise,

265
00:10:47,780 --> 00:10:49,180
then there would be an easy way of winning.

266
00:10:50,280 --> 00:10:53,020
So, the reason why you randomize in the real game is

267
00:10:53,020 --> 00:10:55,040
so people are not able to figure out what you're going to do.

268
00:10:55,040 --> 00:10:56,580
They aren't able to optimally respond

269
00:10:56,580 --> 00:10:57,360
to exactly what you're doing.

270
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:58,960
Instead, you try to be unpredictable,

271
00:10:59,220 --> 00:11:00,260
to leave uncertainty.

272
00:11:00,260 --> 00:11:03,800
So, then they cannot simply do exactly the thing they want

273
00:11:03,800 --> 00:11:04,560
to do in order to beat you.

274
00:11:05,640 --> 00:11:08,800
This is clear, so all in the famous game

275
00:11:08,800 --> 00:11:09,740
of rock, paper, scissors.

276
00:11:10,740 --> 00:11:12,400
Right? So, everyone knows rock, paper, scissors.

277
00:11:13,140 --> 00:11:15,280
Yes, so this is a way where you play rock.

278
00:11:15,820 --> 00:11:18,100
You can play paper, or you can play scissors.

279
00:11:18,700 --> 00:11:22,960
Right? And as you remember, yes, so paper covers rock,

280
00:11:23,460 --> 00:11:25,060
and scissors get paper.

281
00:11:25,380 --> 00:11:26,760
And then the other one, of course,

282
00:11:26,760 --> 00:11:28,140
is rock smashes scissors.

283
00:11:28,700 --> 00:11:28,940
All right.

284
00:11:28,940 --> 00:11:30,220
So, this is the game.

285
00:11:30,740 --> 00:11:31,020
All right.

286
00:11:31,460 --> 00:11:33,320
Now, what would happen in this game

287
00:11:33,320 --> 00:11:34,860
if you always play scissors?

288
00:11:35,220 --> 00:11:38,560
Yes, the other player would say, you always play scissors?

289
00:11:38,840 --> 00:11:40,080
Fine, then I will always play rock,

290
00:11:40,080 --> 00:11:40,840
and I will always beat him.

291
00:11:41,780 --> 00:11:42,040
All right?

292
00:11:42,300 --> 00:11:44,160
And similarly, for anything that you always play,

293
00:11:44,260 --> 00:11:45,540
there is a simple strategy.

294
00:11:45,860 --> 00:11:49,520
So, what is it that just intuitively you want to work

295
00:11:49,520 --> 00:11:51,960
out, what is the mixed strategy Nash Equilibrium for this game?

296
00:11:53,320 --> 00:11:54,620
One-third, one-third, one-third.

297
00:11:54,620 --> 00:11:55,360
That one makes sense.

298
00:11:55,360 --> 00:11:57,140
That's the only one that can't be exploited.

299
00:11:57,140 --> 00:12:00,620
If you did any one of those things more often than a third,

300
00:12:00,620 --> 00:12:02,560
then on average, your opponent would be better off

301
00:12:02,860 --> 00:12:04,680
by just playing the thing that beats that thing,

302
00:12:04,680 --> 00:12:06,340
and they would on average tend to beat you.

303
00:12:07,420 --> 00:12:10,000
Now, weird fact is there actually is a World Series

304
00:12:10,000 --> 00:12:11,420
of rock, paper, scissors, and there are people

305
00:12:11,420 --> 00:12:12,020
who are good at it.

306
00:12:13,160 --> 00:12:13,420
All right?

307
00:12:13,420 --> 00:12:18,220
So, somehow, there are people who can, like,

308
00:12:18,220 --> 00:12:19,360
they don't know who you are.

309
00:12:19,360 --> 00:12:21,600
They look in your eyes, and that guy's going to play rock.

310
00:12:22,200 --> 00:12:25,540
And they are right more often than chance.

311
00:12:25,540 --> 00:12:30,000
I mean, again, I'm very puzzled by this, and I feel

312
00:12:30,000 --> 00:12:33,180
like I can at least get a tie against the world champion just

313
00:12:33,180 --> 00:12:37,180
by going and randomizing on idiosyncratic things,

314
00:12:37,180 --> 00:12:39,340
but maybe I'm wrong, and maybe that guy could crush me.

315
00:12:40,000 --> 00:12:40,580
All right?

316
00:12:40,580 --> 00:12:41,040
I don't know.

317
00:12:41,040 --> 00:12:41,380
But anyway.

318
00:12:41,380 --> 00:12:42,400
A table of random numbers.

319
00:12:42,840 --> 00:12:45,260
Yes. You know, you were like, you were just like,

320
00:12:45,260 --> 00:12:48,060
look around the room, and then, like, I'm going to base this

321
00:12:48,060 --> 00:12:52,120
upon, you know, like, whether or not the seventh person

322
00:12:52,120 --> 00:12:57,220
that I see is going to, like, is going to, I don't know,

323
00:12:57,620 --> 00:13:01,000
like, has, you know, like, two hands on the table,

324
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:02,240
one or zero, you know.

325
00:13:02,240 --> 00:13:04,340
Like, there's so many things that you can base it on,

326
00:13:04,820 --> 00:13:07,300
and it seems like to me, like, each time I can just come

327
00:13:07,300 --> 00:13:08,960
up with a new weird thing to base it on,

328
00:13:08,960 --> 00:13:11,380
and I don't see how the World Series winner could beat me,

329
00:13:11,380 --> 00:13:13,820
but obviously, there's a bunch of people who go against

330
00:13:13,820 --> 00:13:15,440
that guy thinking that they could beat him,

331
00:13:15,440 --> 00:13:16,440
and he beats them, so.

332
00:13:16,880 --> 00:13:18,420
Or that he can't beat them, and he is, so I don't know.

333
00:13:18,420 --> 00:13:22,240
But anyway, but normally, the, you know,

334
00:13:22,240 --> 00:13:24,360
there is a clear equilibrium for rock, paper, scissors,

335
00:13:24,360 --> 00:13:25,760
and that's one-third, one-third, one-third,

336
00:13:26,160 --> 00:13:28,760
and if you don't play that way, and people figure it out,

337
00:13:28,760 --> 00:13:29,320
you will lose.

338
00:13:30,520 --> 00:13:32,380
So, I say this is another, another insight

339
00:13:32,380 --> 00:13:35,600
into why the mixed strategy concept makes sense, right?

340
00:13:36,120 --> 00:13:38,060
Or, you know, here is a real world application.

341
00:13:38,600 --> 00:13:40,100
So, who does better in the world,

342
00:13:40,100 --> 00:13:41,800
hard bargainers or soft bargainers?

343
00:13:42,640 --> 00:13:44,880
All right, now, there's a lot of people here who just want

344
00:13:44,880 --> 00:13:48,380
to answer as if there's a pure strategy answer, right?

345
00:13:48,380 --> 00:13:50,840
So, of course, the popular answer is hard bargaining.

346
00:13:51,080 --> 00:13:53,520
All right, so, hard bargaining wins.

347
00:13:53,700 --> 00:13:56,400
Just go for the jugular and demand everything,

348
00:13:56,620 --> 00:13:58,700
and then everyone will do what you say, and you will win.

349
00:13:59,420 --> 00:14:00,760
All right, a popular answer,

350
00:14:01,120 --> 00:14:04,280
but there is an obvious downside of this approach.

351
00:14:04,880 --> 00:14:06,900
I mean, what if you meet another person like you?

352
00:14:08,060 --> 00:14:09,440
Then there is no cooperation.

353
00:14:09,520 --> 00:14:10,680
Then there is war, right?

354
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:12,000
So, there will be no cooperation.

355
00:14:12,380 --> 00:14:15,340
So, it can't always be the case for everyone

356
00:14:15,340 --> 00:14:16,320
that it's better to do this.

357
00:14:16,320 --> 00:14:18,920
In a world where everyone acted like this horrible person,

358
00:14:19,560 --> 00:14:21,760
you would want to be the one person who just says, sure,

359
00:14:21,760 --> 00:14:24,800
whatever you want, man, it's all cool.

360
00:14:25,120 --> 00:14:27,340
And you would be the one person who would go and trade

361
00:14:27,340 --> 00:14:30,760
with everyone, get a small bit, while they just sit there

362
00:14:30,820 --> 00:14:33,760
by knocking their heads together, failing to cooperate.

363
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:35,780
You would be the one person that could actually make trades.

364
00:14:36,320 --> 00:14:38,200
In that world, you would be the one person

365
00:14:38,660 --> 00:14:42,180
who is the Mr. Cooperative, the hippie, who just says,

366
00:14:42,180 --> 00:14:43,240
I don't need much.

367
00:14:43,240 --> 00:14:45,600
I just, the world is big enough for my needs,

368
00:14:45,600 --> 00:14:47,040
but not big enough for my greed.

369
00:14:47,440 --> 00:14:49,340
Say that kind of thing.

370
00:14:49,340 --> 00:14:51,840
And, of course, it turns out that when I have this attitude,

371
00:14:51,840 --> 00:14:53,440
I'm actually the richest man in the world.

372
00:14:54,760 --> 00:14:56,180
So, you know, keep that in mind.

373
00:14:56,180 --> 00:14:59,780
Now, on the other hand, you also, in a world of hippies,

374
00:14:59,780 --> 00:15:02,000
however, then the very aggressive strategy starts

375
00:15:02,000 --> 00:15:02,480
to pay off.

376
00:15:03,760 --> 00:15:06,640
So, if you want to be the one hippie

377
00:15:06,640 --> 00:15:08,840
in the aggressive world, and the one aggressive person

378
00:15:08,840 --> 00:15:11,460
in the hippie world, what is the,

379
00:15:11,460 --> 00:15:13,760
there is not a pure strategy Nash equilibrium.

380
00:15:13,760 --> 00:15:16,160
There's going to be a mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

381
00:15:16,500 --> 00:15:19,840
where there is a mix of hard and soft bargaining, and some,

382
00:15:19,840 --> 00:15:23,920
and the marginal person is going to be just indifferent

383
00:15:23,920 --> 00:15:25,020
between the two strategies.

384
00:15:25,520 --> 00:15:28,080
And that is a prediction that makes a lot of sense.

385
00:15:28,880 --> 00:15:32,420
So, I do have a pet theory that actually,

386
00:15:32,900 --> 00:15:34,060
despite all this logic,

387
00:15:34,060 --> 00:15:35,920
in the real world soft bargaining actually is

388
00:15:35,920 --> 00:15:40,480
on average a better strategy, because so many human attitudes

389
00:15:40,480 --> 00:15:42,740
for a strategy are reformed during,

390
00:15:42,740 --> 00:15:45,760
when we're in primitive tribes, when there's a big payoff

391
00:15:45,760 --> 00:15:47,400
to knowing that, for people knowing

392
00:15:47,400 --> 00:15:48,400
that you're tough and aggressive.

393
00:15:48,400 --> 00:15:50,780
You know, there's only 20 people, and you're Mr. Tough.

394
00:15:51,160 --> 00:15:53,900
People get used to, well, that guy, you know, he's pushy,

395
00:15:53,900 --> 00:15:55,200
and so you better just give him what he wants.

396
00:15:55,760 --> 00:15:58,140
But in the real world, almost of that,

397
00:15:58,140 --> 00:15:59,940
most of that benefit has gone away.

398
00:15:59,940 --> 00:16:01,540
We're dealing with strangers all the time

399
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:02,720
that we'll never deal with again.

400
00:16:03,020 --> 00:16:04,620
There's no point in trying to impress them

401
00:16:04,620 --> 00:16:06,020
about how tough we are.

402
00:16:06,020 --> 00:16:07,400
They don't, they're never going to remember us.

403
00:16:07,400 --> 00:16:08,340
We'll never see them again.

404
00:16:08,340 --> 00:16:11,600
In a world like that, actually, you want to be the,

405
00:16:11,600 --> 00:16:14,560
if other people are still acting like they're cavemen times,

406
00:16:14,560 --> 00:16:15,440
and you're the one person

407
00:16:15,440 --> 00:16:18,360
who realizes this is the modern world, I'm never going

408
00:16:18,360 --> 00:16:22,560
to see you again, then the person who is more cooperative

409
00:16:22,560 --> 00:16:23,940
and less aggressive will do better.

410
00:16:24,580 --> 00:16:27,740
So, yes, while there are situations,

411
00:16:27,740 --> 00:16:31,120
while being aggressive is advantageous, I do,

412
00:16:31,120 --> 00:16:34,100
my view is if you do look around in the real modern world,

413
00:16:34,100 --> 00:16:36,360
you will see that it is generally a bad idea.

414
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:38,320
You know, especially if you see a little boy

415
00:16:38,320 --> 00:16:41,180
who is super aggressive, it is not reasonable to think

416
00:16:41,180 --> 00:16:42,260
that kid is going to do well in life.

417
00:16:43,260 --> 00:16:46,200
He could, but it's also very likely he's just going

418
00:16:46,200 --> 00:16:48,480
to end up in jail, right?

419
00:16:48,480 --> 00:16:51,500
A very reasonable thing to predict for a kid

420
00:16:51,500 --> 00:16:54,700
who is really, really pushy and aggressive, and of course,

421
00:16:54,700 --> 00:16:56,560
you know, if he resorts to physical violence, you know,

422
00:16:56,560 --> 00:16:58,820
a lot of kids grow out of it, but then some don't.

423
00:16:59,440 --> 00:17:01,540
And the modern world is very little tolerant

424
00:17:01,540 --> 00:17:02,380
for people like that.

425
00:17:02,380 --> 00:17:05,080
And if you say, well, I'll like beat the mummy out of everybody,

426
00:17:05,080 --> 00:17:07,200
that is not a viable strategy in the real world.

427
00:17:07,200 --> 00:17:11,500
So, you know, try, but it's just going to end in jail.

428
00:17:11,780 --> 00:17:13,420
Maybe I'll be the one guy who never gets caught.

429
00:17:14,060 --> 00:17:16,940
I don't know, I suppose, but you keep throwing the dice,

430
00:17:17,200 --> 00:17:18,400
eventually you're going to end up in jail.

431
00:17:19,700 --> 00:17:22,720
So anyway, so, but the general intuition

432
00:17:22,720 --> 00:17:24,920
of which strategy is the good strategy,

433
00:17:25,240 --> 00:17:28,220
the obvious answer is neither is the good strategy.

434
00:17:28,220 --> 00:17:30,420
It all depends upon the mix in the population,

435
00:17:30,780 --> 00:17:33,980
and in equilibrium, the martial person is doing equally well,

436
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:35,220
either way, right?

437
00:17:35,220 --> 00:17:38,060
And otherwise, again, if hard bargaining was just always just

438
00:17:38,060 --> 00:17:39,620
better for everyone, everyone would do it,

439
00:17:39,620 --> 00:17:40,760
and then it wouldn't be good anymore.

440
00:17:43,060 --> 00:17:44,320
Okay, questions about that?

441
00:17:47,380 --> 00:17:47,680
All right.

442
00:17:47,940 --> 00:17:50,680
Oh, yes, and another time that I use, that I use mixed strategy is

443
00:17:50,680 --> 00:17:52,360
when I am picking exam questions.

444
00:17:53,400 --> 00:17:55,500
So my goal is to give you incentives

445
00:17:55,500 --> 00:17:56,460
to learn all the material.

446
00:17:57,480 --> 00:17:58,660
There is not enough time for me

447
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:00,360
to actually ask questions about all the material.

448
00:18:01,360 --> 00:18:04,080
Therefore, if you could figure out what I'm going to ask,

449
00:18:04,080 --> 00:18:06,140
you could actually learn only a very small amount,

450
00:18:06,880 --> 00:18:09,300
and then you, like, you could spend hardly any time

451
00:18:09,300 --> 00:18:10,820
in this class, you just learn the kinds

452
00:18:10,820 --> 00:18:11,680
of questions I'm going to ask.

453
00:18:11,680 --> 00:18:12,680
You could read my mind.

454
00:18:13,040 --> 00:18:15,660
You could actually get an A while doing almost no work.

455
00:18:16,860 --> 00:18:18,360
But I don't want to let you do that.

456
00:18:18,820 --> 00:18:21,060
So when I am writing, when I'm writing the questions,

457
00:18:21,060 --> 00:18:23,240
I'm there thinking, okay, I need to be unpredictable now.

458
00:18:23,900 --> 00:18:25,640
Okay, I don't want to ask the obvious questions.

459
00:18:26,200 --> 00:18:28,100
So a lot of times I'll go back to past exams

460
00:18:28,100 --> 00:18:29,780
and say what kinds of questions have I asked before?

461
00:18:29,780 --> 00:18:31,600
All right, I'm not going to ask those questions again.

462
00:18:31,600 --> 00:18:32,600
I'm going to ask different questions.

463
00:18:32,600 --> 00:18:35,000
But then sometimes I say that's just what the students think I'm

464
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:35,300
going to do.

465
00:18:35,300 --> 00:18:37,440
So then maybe I'll ask exactly the kind

466
00:18:37,440 --> 00:18:38,900
of question I asked before once in a while.

467
00:18:39,240 --> 00:18:41,680
Not often, but sometimes, just to keep them on their feet.

468
00:18:41,680 --> 00:18:44,300
And this is exactly what I am consciously thinking

469
00:18:44,300 --> 00:18:48,240
about as I write the exam, is trying to randomize the questions

470
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:51,540
so that you do the strategy that is best for dealing

471
00:18:51,540 --> 00:18:54,820
with my strategy, namely to actually understand the material.

472
00:18:54,820 --> 00:18:58,380
Yeah, so I'm doing the mixed strategy.

473
00:18:58,380 --> 00:19:01,420
In order to pressure you to do the unmixed strategy,

474
00:19:01,420 --> 00:19:03,100
you're just learning all the material.

475
00:19:03,420 --> 00:19:06,260
So, yes, is it possible to have a mixed strategy

476
00:19:06,260 --> 00:19:07,860
in Nash equilibrium where one person mixes

477
00:19:07,860 --> 00:19:09,040
and the other person doesn't?

478
00:19:09,360 --> 00:19:10,260
Yeah, it's totally possible.

479
00:19:12,500 --> 00:19:13,800
Okay, all right, so anyway,

480
00:19:14,040 --> 00:19:16,040
using this mixed strategy Nash equilibrium,

481
00:19:16,340 --> 00:19:18,100
it cuts through all this complexity

482
00:19:18,100 --> 00:19:19,480
of unlimited second guessing.

483
00:19:19,860 --> 00:19:21,920
Where again, like in that Popeye criminal thing,

484
00:19:21,920 --> 00:19:25,000
you might start saying, well, he thinks that I think,

485
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,500
that he thinks that I think, that he thinks that I think,

486
00:19:27,500 --> 00:19:31,220
that he's not getting off, so I'm going to get on.

487
00:19:32,560 --> 00:19:35,600
All right, but whenever you see this in a movie,

488
00:19:35,600 --> 00:19:38,520
this all makes a lot of sense until you realize, wait a second,

489
00:19:38,520 --> 00:19:41,500
what if he does the same reasoning with one more step,

490
00:19:41,500 --> 00:19:42,620
then you get the opposite answer.

491
00:19:43,240 --> 00:19:44,860
And there's no natural limit

492
00:19:44,860 --> 00:19:47,340
to how many steps this reasoning should go onto, right?

493
00:19:47,920 --> 00:19:50,480
Like why is seven the right number?

494
00:19:51,400 --> 00:19:54,940
All right, now you might say, well,

495
00:19:54,940 --> 00:19:56,940
maybe empirically there's a number that people do,

496
00:19:56,940 --> 00:19:58,540
but of course, once you ask that,

497
00:19:58,540 --> 00:20:27,900
then you're no longer doing normal game theory.


